WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Provenance is necessary, but it is not enough by itself.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:17.960
A model can have clear origin records, a reviewed license, and strong integrity evidence while still being unsuitable for a particular task, environment, latency requirement, policy constraint, or user population.

3
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.400
Behavioral trust requires evaluation against intended use.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:33.380
The team should test representative prompts, expected inputs, workflow constraints, output formats, refusal expectations, domain terminology, and known failure modes.

5
00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.040
The goal is not to prove perfection.

6
00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:41.580
The goal is to understand whether the model is fit for the job and what review boundaries are required.

7
00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:50.580
Baselines and regression checks are especially important after fine-tuning, conversion, quantization, adapter merges, or model replacement.

8
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.080
A transformation can improve deployment fit while changing behavior.

9
00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:01.020
Without repeatable checks, teams may notice quality problems only after users depend on the model.

10
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:08.340
Safety and policy evaluations should be documented so approval is based on evidence instead of informal impressions.

11
00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:22.040
Useful records include the dataset or prompt set, scoring method, reviewer role, environment, model version, notable failures, known limitations, and conditions attached to approval.

12
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.960
Trust is strongest when provenance evidence and behavioral evidence agree.

13
00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:36.260
The team knows where the checkpoint came from, can verify which file is being used, and has evaluation results showing that the model performs acceptably in the intended workflow.

14
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:42.120
That combination is far more defensible than a one-time download followed by optimistic deployment.
